## 3. Conditional bulk-to-boundary functor

Let the additional boundary package be

\[
\mathcal B=
\left(
j_s,\ {\rm coorientation},\ \kappa_s,\
\nabla_s^\partial,\ T_{\rm end},\ \tau_s
\right),
\tag{3.1}
\]

where:

- \(j_s:\Sigma\to Y_4\) is a smooth family of compact cooriented
  hypersurface embeddings;
- \(\kappa_s\) is a compatible collar/productization, or an equivalent fixed
  adapted-operator convention;
- \(\nabla_s^\partial\) is the restricted physical SpinC/q79 connection
  history;
- \(T_{\rm end}\) is the endpoint comparison when the family is a loop only
  modulo gauge;
- \(\tau_s\) is the zero-mode taming or BFV polarization.

The coorientation and bulk metric determine the unit normal \(\nu_s\). The
boundary Clifford bundle is

\[
E_s=j_s^*(S\otimes F_{\rm q79}).
\tag{3.2}
\]

For tangential covectors \(\xi\), the adapted boundary symbol is

\[
\sigma_{B_s}(x,\xi)
=
\sigma_D(x,\nu_s)^{-1}\sigma_D(x,\xi).
\tag{3.3}
\]

With the declared product-collar convention,

\[
D_{\rm q79}
=
\sigma_D(\nu_s)\left(\partial_r+B_s\right).
\tag{3.4}
\]

Thus \(D_{\rm q79}\) and \(\mathcal B\) determine:

\[
P_s^{\rm APS}=\mathbf 1_{(-\infty,0)}(B_s),
\tag{3.5}
\]

with zero modes assigned by \(\tau_s\), and the boundary Green/BFV form

\[
\beta_s(\phi,\psi)
=
\int_\Sigma
\left\langle
\sigma_D(\nu_s)\phi,\psi
\right\rangle\,d\mu_s.
\tag{3.6}
\]

Equation (3.6) is understood up to the standard overall sign or factor of
\(i\) fixed by the declared Green-form convention.

At an isolated regular crossing \(s_*\), the crossing space is

\[
K_{s_*}=\ker B_{s_*}.
\tag{3.7}
\]

Spin-lift, connection and coorientation preserving ambient isotopies conjugate
this complete package. Therefore the map

\[
(Y_4,g,e,D_{\rm q79};\mathcal B)
\longmapsto
(E_s,B_s,P_s^{\rm APS},\beta_s,K_{s_*})
\tag{3.8}
\]

is canonical at the declared analytic tier. It is a functor from supplied
boundary geometry, not a rule selecting \(\mathcal B\).

## 4. Exact continuum crossing

Use \(S^1_{\rm shared}\) only as a compact holonomy/Fourier model. It is not
physical time and is not the full three-dimensional spatial boundary.

On periodic spinors, define

\[
B_a=-i\frac{d}{d\theta}+a.
\tag{4.1}
\]

For

\[
\phi_n(\theta)=e^{in\theta},
\qquad n\in\mathbb Z,
\]

one has the exact spectrum

\[
B_a\phi_n=(n+a)\phi_n.
\tag{4.2}
\]

Take the unit-winding path

\[
a(u)=\frac12+u,
\qquad 0\leq u\leq1.
\tag{4.3}
\]

Both endpoints have holonomy \(-1\). They are gauge equivalent because, for
\(U(\theta)=e^{i\theta}\),

\[
U B_{3/2}U^{-1}=B_{1/2}.
\tag{4.4}
\]

Only the mode \(n=-1\) crosses zero:

\[
\lambda_{-1}(a)=a-1.
\tag{4.5}
\]

Indeed,

\[
n\leq-2
\Longrightarrow
n+a\leq-\frac12<0,
\]

and

\[
n\geq0
\Longrightarrow
n+a\geq\frac12>0.
\]

At \(a=1\),

\[
K_{\rm circ}
=
\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb C}(e^{-i\theta}),
\qquad
\Gamma|_{K_{\rm circ}}=+1.
\tag{4.6}
\]

Consequently,

\[
\operatorname{sf}(B_{a(u)})=+1.
\tag{4.7}
\]

This is a continuum differential-operator realization of the simple crossing,
not a selected physical q79 boundary history.

## 5. Exact Galerkin reduction

Set

\[
t=2(a-1),
\qquad -1\leq t\leq1.
\tag{5.1}
\]

Restrict \(B_a\) to the Fourier modes

\[
n=-3,-1,2.
\]

The exact Galerkin family is

\[
G(t)
=
\operatorname{diag}
\left(
-2+\frac t2,\,
\frac t2,\,
3+\frac t2
\right).
\tag{5.2}
\]

For \(0\leq r\leq1\), define

\[
H_r(t)
=
\operatorname{diag}
\left(
-2+\frac{(1-r)t}{2},\,
\frac{1+r}{2}t,\,
3+\frac{(1-r)t}{2}
\right).
\tag{5.3}
\]

Then

\[
H_0(t)=G(t),
\qquad
H_1(t)=\operatorname{diag}(-2,t,3).
\tag{5.4}
\]

Throughout the homotopy,

\[
-2+\frac{(1-r)t}{2}\leq-\frac32<0,
\]

\[
3+\frac{(1-r)t}{2}\geq\frac52>0,
\]

and

\[
\frac{1+r}{2}>0.
\]

No spectator mode crosses zero and the central crossing remains regular and
positive. Hence the finite witness and the continuum Galerkin family have the
same spectral flow \(+1\). The finite witness is no longer an arbitrary matrix:
it is an exact spectrally flattened local model of (4.1).

## 6. Exact boundary-path nonselection

At the same sign-holonomy basepoint, compare:

\[
a_0(u)=\frac12
\]

and

\[
a_1(u)=\frac12+u
\quad\text{modulo the endpoint gauge map }U.
\]

The first path has spectral flow zero. The second has spectral flow one.
They use the same:

- circle metric;
- periodic spin structure;
- q79 sign-holonomy basepoint;
- endpoint gauge class.

Therefore endpoint sign holonomy and bulk geometry do not determine the
classifying loop, its spectral flow or its crossing line.

`A_causal` can orient an already supplied physical evolution. It does not
identify compact circle phase with noncompact physical time, and it does not
emit the missing boundary loop. Thus:

```text
B.QFT.02_bulk_only_boundary_family_selection
  = excluded_by_same_basepoint_sf_zero_vs_one_counterfamily.
```

